## Matching Ranges of Values

Just as `for` loops use ranges (`0..10`), `match` can use ranges as patterns:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let score = 85;

  let grade = match score {
    90..101 => "A",
    80..90 => "B",
    70..80 => "C",
    60..70 => "D",
    _ => "F",
  };

  io.println(grade);  // prints: B
  return 0;
}
```

### Range Rules in Match

Ranges in match follow the same rules as `for` ranges: **inclusive start, exclusive end**:

```xiom
match value {
  0..10 => "0 through 9",    // matches 0,1,2,...,9
  10..20 => "10 through 19",  // matches 10,11,...,19
  20..30 => "20 through 29",  // matches 20,21,...,29
}
```

### Practical Range Matching

```xiom
// Price tiers
match price {
  0..10 => "Budget",
  10..50 => "Standard",
  50..200 => "Premium",
  _ => "Luxury",
}

// Age categories
match age {
  0..2 => "Baby",
  2..13 => "Child",
  13..20 => "Teenager",
  20..65 => "Adult",
  _ => "Senior",
}

// Time of day
match hour {
  0..6 => "Night",
  6..12 => "Morning",
  12..18 => "Afternoon",
  18..24 => "Evening",
  _ => "Invalid",
}
```

### Ranges vs Guards

```xiom
// Range pattern — cleaner for contiguous buckets
match score {
  90..101 => "A",
  80..90 => "B",
  _ => "Other",
}

// Guard — needed for non-contiguous or complex conditions
match score {
  s if s >= 90 && s < 95 => "A-",
  s if s >= 95 => "A",
  _ => "Other",
}
```

Use ranges when values form contiguous groups. Use guards for everything else.

